\author{utensil}
\date{2026-08-27}

\p{These are research notes for \strong{Formalized Theoretical Intelligence
Potential} (FTIP), a programme about the capability that can be obtained from
a pretrained language model by post-training. The question is comparative.
Under a specified evaluation procedure and resource budget, how much does a
post-training procedure change what the model can reliably do?}

\p{FTIP does not assign an intrinsic intelligence number to a model. The
quantity under study depends on the base model, the information supplied
during training, the permitted procedures, and the evaluation. The current
draft develops the language needed to state those dependencies. It does not
yet assert a scaling law or a final theorem.}

\p{The exposition begins with tokens, probability laws, and the decoder-only
Transformer. It then treats pretraining, supervised and preference
post-training, and reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards. Tasks,
environments, and agent histories appear only after those training stages have
been introduced. The final sections define independent evaluation and record
the questions that later theorem drafts must answer.}

\p{Definitions imported from a paper cite that paper in the same tree. A
standard specialization names the standard object being specialized. Objects
introduced for this programme are marked \strong{FTIP synthesis} or
\strong{FTIP proposal}. Those labels prevent a convenient research interface
from being mistaken for an established result.}

